Geneva library - Examples
=========================

This directory contains some examples of how to use the Geneva library for
performing parametric optimization.

If you are looking for a starting point for your own work, use the quickstart
in ../quickstart instead: it is a self-contained project that consumes an
*installed* Geneva via find_package(Geneva) and can be copied out of this tree
as-is. The examples below are built as part of the Geneva tree and illustrate
individual aspects of the library.

Every example program's main() has one of two forms (the manual's chapter on GOptimizer
explains them):
o The short form: Gem::Geneva::runOptimization(argc, argv, {.produce = ...}). Geneva's
  description is handed to courtier's launcher, which owns the command line, the consumer and
  the role of the process; the producing function runs on the submitter only, with a
  GOptimizer built for it. This is the form to use unless the program sets something on the launcher.
  01_GSimpleOptimizer shows it.
o The long form: the program builds Geneva's description (GOptimizationDescription) and
  courtier's launcher (Gem::Courtier::GLauncherT<GGenome>) in main() and hands the producing
  function to launcher.run(description, {...}). It is needed when something is set on the
  launcher before it runs -- a consumer chosen in code (11_GExternalEvaluator), a module path,
  the item processor of a client (16_GMPISubClients). 02_GParaboloid2D shows it next to 01.
A program needs neither form if it builds its consumer itself, without a launcher
(06_GDirect*, 15_GMPIConsumer): courtier's buildConsumer() and buildConsumerClient() find the
consumer in its module like the launcher does.

The following examples are provided:

00_GQuickStart:
---------------

A short but complete program: one problem class with two parameters and a known
minimum, handed to GOptimizer, searched, plotted and read out. The manual's Quick Start
chapter walks through it line by line; start here.

01_GSimpleOptimizer:
--------------------

This example is meant as the first starting point for exploring Geneva. It provides
easy command line access to the minimization of various mathematical functions, through
different algorithms. Its main() has the short form (runOptimization()); 02_GParaboloid2D
shows the long form.

02_GParaboloid2D:
-----------------

The main purpose of this example is to provide users with a first insight into
how an optimization problem may be specified in Geneva. You should thus particularly
look at the GParaboloidIndividual2D class. The main function illustrates additional
ways of specifying the optimization algorithms to be used, and how individuals can
be added to the optimization driver GOptimizer. Its main() has the long form: Geneva's
description and courtier's launcher are built in main(); 01_GSimpleOptimizer shows the short form.

03_GParameterObjectUsagePatterns:
---------------------------------

This sample program illustrates the usage of Geneva's parameter objects.
GParameterObject-derivatives are the main vehicle through which optimization
parameters are specified. There is a whole family of parameter objects.

04_GMultiCriterionParabola:
